/**
 * Simplified country outlines, for the loading screen.
 *
 * GENERATED — do not hand-edit. Built from Natural Earth (public domain) in
 * two passes, because one scale cannot serve both Russia and Monaco.
 *
 * The 169 countries the 110m file draws usefully come from it, reduced with
 * Douglas–Peucker at a tolerance scaled to each country's own span, so a small
 * country keeps its shape instead of collapsing to a triangle, and rounded to
 * one decimal.
 *
 * The remaining 31 — microstates and island nations that 110m omits or draws
 * as a speck — come from the 10m file, where every threshold has to scale:
 * Monaco spans 0.07 degrees and keeps five decimals, Kiribati spans two and
 * keeps three. For the archipelagos only the recognisable main cluster is
 * kept: rings are dropped beyond eight times the main island's own size from
 * it, because the alternative is honest and unreadable — all of Micronesia is
 * real, but its islands are 2500km apart and the frame would hold eight
 * two-pixel dots. So Micronesia is Pohnpei, Kiribati is the Line Islands,
 * Mauritius is Mauritius without Rodrigues, and the Maldives keep the chain.
 *
 * Coordinates are plain longitude/latitude with the sign of y flipped, so the
 * paths read as an equirectangular map with north up and need no projection
 * at render time. `box` is that country's bounding box, which is what lets one
 * path serve both the faint world map and the zoomed outline.
 *
 * One country has no entry: Natural Earth's Vatican is a seven-point
 * placeholder 147 metres across, well under the real thing, and drawing it
 * means inventing a shape and rendering it at a zoom the rasteriser cannot
 * hold. The loading screen falls back to the map, the flag and the name.
 *
 * Kept out of the main bundle: `CountryOutline` is lazy-loaded, so this only
 * arrives when someone first opens a country.
 */
export interface Outline {
  /** SVG path data, already in screen orientation. */
  d: string
  /** [minX, minY, width, height] in the same units. */
  box: [number, number, number, number]
  /**
   * Sub-pixel on the world map behind the outline. Drawing the path there
   * would cost bytes and show nothing, so the map marks these with a dot
   * instead and they are left out of `WORLD_PATH`.
   */
  small?: true
  /**
   * Crosses the antimeridian. `d` runs past 180 so the country stays one
   * shape rather than two clusters pinned to opposite edges of the map.
   */
  wrap?: true
}

